solution.p:
Now all the ways to distribute the 14 units to the four y_i variables can be found using stars and bars, specifically 14 stars and 3 bars, so {17 \choose 14} ways. But this includes the ways that one or more y_i variables can be assigned more than 3 units. So subtract using PIE to get

\displaystyle{                {17 \choose 3} -\left( {4\choose 1} {11 \choose 3}-{4\choose 2} {5 \choose 3} + \dots \right)
              }
